<section class="syllabus-hero">
    <div class="container text-center">
        <h1>Microservices Masterclass with ASP.NET Core and Azure Cloud</h1>
        <p>💡 Powered by ItTechGenie</p>
        <p>📅 Course Duration: 2.5 Months</p>
        <div class="syllabus-highlight">
            <span class="syllabus-pill">GIT Repository Access – Daily sample coding projects</span>
            <span class="syllabus-pill">Recorded Sessions – Access anytime for revision</span>
            <span class="syllabus-pill">Interview Questions & MCQs – For every chapter</span>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="container">
    <div class="syllabus-card">
        <h2>🧩 Module 1: Microservices Fundamentals</h2>

        <h3>Chapter 1: Application Architectures Overview</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>What You Will Learn:</strong> Explore the evolution of application architectures—from monolithic to microservices—using real-world examples like Netflix.</li>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Application overview, Monolithic vs Microservices, Use cases (Netflix, Amazon, Uber), pros/cons, migration triggers.</li>
            <li><strong>Key Terminologies:</strong> Tightly Coupled, Loosely Coupled, Scalability, Service Boundaries, DDD.</li>
            <li><strong>Scenario:</strong> Food delivery app migration improved uptime and scalability.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Compare monolithic and microservices architectures using diagrams.</li>
            <li><strong>Quick Quiz:</strong> Deployment ease, monolith risks, Netflix adoption, scalability, readiness signs.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 2: ASP.NET Core Web API Fundamentals</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>What You Will Learn:</strong> Build your first Web API using ASP.NET Core.</li>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> .NET Core vs .NET Framework, setup, controllers, routing, models/DTOs/DI, EF Core CRUD, Postman, Swagger.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Student Management CRUD API with seeding.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 3: Core Microservices Design Principles</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Characteristics, Loose coupling, SRP, Bounded Context, DDD intro.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Identify bounded contexts for online shopping platform.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 4: Structuring Microservices Projects</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Clean structure, layered organization, repository + unit of work, API versioning.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Refactor Student Management API into clean structure.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 5: Building Your First Microservices</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Product & Order services, separate databases, repository pattern, Swagger, unit testing.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Build Product and Order services with tests.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 6: Inter-Service Communication Basics</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> REST best practices, HTTP status codes, error handling, async communication.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Connect Product and Order services via HttpClient.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 7: Data Management Strategies</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Database per service, eventual consistency, distributed transactions, saga pattern, 2PC.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Simulate order placement using saga steps.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 8: API Gateway Fundamentals</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> API gateway purpose, Ocelot setup, routing, aggregation, basic security.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Implement Ocelot gateway for Product and Order services.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 9: Basic Caching with Redis</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Caching fundamentals, Redis, distributed caching, cache invalidation and eviction policies.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Add Redis caching to Product Service list queries.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 10: Authentication and Authorization Basics</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Authentication vs authorization, JWT auth, role-based access, policies.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Secure Product Service with JWT roles.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>🧩 Module 3: Advanced Microservices Concepts</h2>

        <h3>Chapter 11: Messaging Fundamentals</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Sync vs async messaging, RabbitMQ setup, queues vs streams.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Order placed notification with RabbitMQ.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 12: Introduction to Docker</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Containers vs VMs, Docker setup, Dockerfiles, running containers.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Containerize Product and Order services.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 13: Docker Compose Basics</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Compose intro, multi-container setup, networking, env variables.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> docker-compose for Product, Order, SQL Server.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 14: Advanced Service Communication – gRPC</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> REST vs gRPC, gRPC communication, bidirectional streaming.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> gRPC service for real-time order updates.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 15: Event-Driven Architecture</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Events vs commands vs queries, event bus, event sourcing.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Product Added event with event bus.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 16: Using MassTransit for Advanced Messaging</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> MassTransit setup, retries, fault handling, routing.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Replace RabbitMQ code with MassTransit integration.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 17: CQRS and Event Sourcing Basics</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> CQRS intro, command/query separation, event sourcing basics.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Build CQRS pattern in Order Service.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 18: Microservices Security Best Practices</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> OAuth2 and OIDC, IdentityServer, securing APIs.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Create simple Identity Server.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 19: Caching Advanced Techniques</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Cache-aside, write-through/behind, Redis Pub/Sub.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Redis Pub/Sub for cache invalidation.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 20: Resilience and Failure Handling</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Retry, timeout, circuit breaker, chaos engineering, exponential backoff.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Polly policies around HttpClient calls.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 21: Observability and Monitoring</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Serilog + ELK, OpenTelemetry, metrics with Prometheus/Grafana, alerting.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Structured logging with Serilog + Seq/ELK.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 22: Kubernetes and Container Orchestration</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Pods, services, deployments, AKS/Minikube, Helm.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Deploy Product/Order services with YAML.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 23: Real-Time Communication in Microservices</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> WebSockets, SignalR, real-time features.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> SignalR notification on new order.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 24: Advanced CI/CD Pipelines</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> CI/CD fundamentals, Azure DevOps/GitHub Actions, automation.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Pipeline for build, test, deploy.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 25: Clean Architecture and Advanced DDD</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Layers, domain events, aggregates, value objects.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Refactor services into Clean Architecture layers.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 26: Steeltoe Framework for Microservices</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Steeltoe, config server, discovery, health checks.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Integrate Steeltoe Config Server and Discovery Client.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 27: Service Mesh and Serverless Architectures</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Istio/Linkerd basics, Azure Functions/AWS Lambda, hybrid architectures.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Serverless order notification with Azure Functions.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 28: GraphQL in Microservices</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> GraphQL vs REST, schema, queries, mutations.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Product Query API with GraphQL.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 29: Chaos Engineering and Fault Injection</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Chaos testing basics, simulating failures, tools.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Manual network disruptions to analyze behavior.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 30: Performance Tuning and Advanced Observability</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Load testing, profiling tools, tracing, Grafana dashboards.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 31: Modern Deployment Strategies</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Blue-green, canary, GitOps, operators.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Canary deployment for Product Service.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>🧩 Module 4: Azure-Native Microservices Architecture</h2>

        <h3>Chapter 32: Hosting Microservices on Azure App Services</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Azure App Services, deployments, App Configuration, Key Vault.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Deploy Product/Order to Azure with secure config.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 33: Azure SQL Database for Microservices</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Azure SQL basics, connectivity, scaling, backups.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Move Product DB to Azure SQL with Key Vault connection string.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 34: Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS)</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> AKS setup, ACR, autoscaling, resource limits.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Push images to ACR and deploy with Helm.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 35: Azure Event Grid and Azure Service Bus</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Event Grid, Service Bus, fan-out patterns.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Replace RabbitMQ order notification with Azure Service Bus/Event Grid.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 36: Azure Monitor, Log Analytics, and App Insights</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Azure Monitor logging, Application Insights tracing, alerts.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Enable App Insights and create metrics/alerts.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 37: Azure API Management (APIM)</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> APIM vs Ocelot, rate limiting, policies, JWT validation.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Secure and publish APIs with APIM.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Chapter 38: Serverless Integration with Azure Functions</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Topics Covered:</strong> Azure Functions, Event Grid/HTTP/Timer triggers, Durable Functions.</li>
            <li><strong>Example:</strong> Event Grid-triggered function for order notifications.</li>
        </ul>

